Hi [~accountid:614c2822be1de8006be956c4],

Please find below the external counsel answers to my questions regarding the tip functionality:

Considering the above-described procedure, could you please let us know +*whether*+ :

* The above-mentioned functionality as described in the attachment can be implemented in Portugal; 

{color:#ff5630}DLA: Yes, the functionality can be implemented in Portugal.{color}

*  There are legal restrictions in Portugal to ask users to tip couriers before an order has been delivered;

{color:#ff5630}DLA: No, provided it remains clear that the tip is entirely voluntary and that the possibility of submitting an order is not made conditional on granting a tip.{color}

*  Bolt can allow the user to set up a default tip amount for orders once and charge them without asking for confirmation in each new order;

{color:#ff5630}DLA: No. Article 4(1)(e) of Decree-Law 24/2014, of February 14, which has transposed the EU consumers’ Directive 2011/83/EU, determines that “before the consumer is bound by the contract or offer, the trader must provide the consumer, in a clear and comprehensible manner, information on the total price of the good or service, including taxes and fees, supplementary transportation charges, postal or delivery costs {color}{color:#ff5630}+or any other charges that may apply+{color}{color:#ff5630}.” We would argue that the tip pertains to the category of “other charges that may apply” and we consider very doubtful that Bolt manages to prove (the burden of proof is incumbent upon the trader) that the consumer was informed in a clear and comprehensible manner about the tip in respect of each single transaction (“contract”) where confirmation is {color}{color:#ff5630}+not+{color}{color:#ff5630} sought for each new order.{color}

* There are legal restrictions to ask users to tip couriers (or edit/cancel the tip amount which has been charged earlier) for the historical order which has been delivered a few days ago?

{color:#ff5630}DLA: No, provided it remains clear that the tip is entirely voluntary and that the time span is reasonable such as to ensure that the customer can reasonably track the transaction at stake.{color}

*  We must update any of our Agreements (Individual Courier, Courier fleet, Restaurant) to implement the Courier tips feature;

{color:#ff5630}DLA: Please see our answer to q. 6. To the extent necessary, we recommend to include similar provisions in any relevant agreements.{color}

*  We must update any of our T&C (Eater, Individual Courier, Courier fleet, Restaurant) to implement the courier tips feature;

{color:#ff5630}DLA: Yes. We suggest to include two additional provisions in both the T&C for Clients (section 6) and the T&C for Couriers (section 7):{color}

{color:#ff5630} {color}

{color:#ff5630}“The Courier Fee does not include tips. The Client is free to tip the Courier through the Bolt Food Platform by using the mechanism provided by Bolt, in which case Bolt will collect and deliver the tip to the Courier (either the individual courier or the fleet owner, depending on the case) on behalf of the Client. Bolt does not charge any commission on tips granted by the Client.”.{color}

{color:#ff5630} {color}

{color:#ff5630}“The Client may totally or partially revoke the tip granted through the Bolt Food Platform within a period of \[•] from the moment the tip is granted. In such case, the revoked amount will be refunded by Bolt to the Client.”{color}

{color:#ff5630} {color}

{color:#ff5630}Insofar as restaurants are not involved in the tips paid by clients to the couriers, we understand that no amendments to the T&C for Partners are necessary.{color}

*  There are legal restrictions to allow users to refund tips through our customer services or automatically;

{color:#ff5630}DLA: Generally speaking, there are no legal restrictions. However, rights and legitimate expectations of the couriers to the tips are created at the moment they are informed about the fact that they have been granted a tip. Therefore, any action that deprives a courier from a tip in respect of which a right or a legitimate expectation has been created seems problematic and may give rise to disputes grounded on breach of rights/legitimate expectations and/or misappropriation of funds. The legally safest way to allow for refunds is within a limited period of time after the tip has been granted and before the courier is informed about the tip.{color}

* We may show customers the following message “Couriers get 100% of the tips“(our competitors normally use that sentence) considering tips may be subject to courier fleet commissions and some couriers may have to pay taxes on the amount of the tip received. Please note that bank charges will be borne by Bolt.

{color:#ff5630}DLA: Such a statement would not be accurate in the case of courier fleets and should therefore not be used. It would be preferable to use a statement along the lines of “Bolt passes on 100% of the tip” or “Bolt will not charge any commission on the tip”.{color}

* There are legal risks associated with the fact that the amount of tips will vary from city to city and will be set by our local operations teams;

{color:#ff5630}DLA: No, provided it remains clear that the tip is entirely voluntary.{color}

*  There are any risks associated with refunding  tips ?

 {color:#ff5630}DLA: see above reply to question nr. 7.{color}

*  There are any further legal considerations/implications we must take into account.

{color:#ff5630}DLA: VAT and income tax considerations, although the applicable regime is pretty equivalent across most EU countries.{color}

----

Should you further questions, please do let me know.

Thank you\!

