Hey\!

This is a bit tricky question as there are several aspects we should consider:

# TÖR is only acceptable for natural persons. If they have any form of legal entity, they don’t have an obligation to state the relationship in TÖR.
# TÖR principles are mostly regulated herein:  

# "*employment*" means the performance of work on the basis of a contract of employment or a contract under a law of obligations, public service within the meaning of § 5 of the Public Service Act, the performance of work without remuneration in the economic interests of a company or a self-employed person and the provision of services or the sale of goods referred to in § 1 of the Simplified Taxation of Business Income Act;
# "*person performing work*" means a natural person who performs work within the meaning of point 1 of this paragraph;
# "*person providing work*" means an Estonian resident or non-resident legal person, an Estonian state authority or local government body, a natural person or a self-employed person who concludes an agreement on the basis of which work is provided or appoints a person referred to in point 2 of this paragraph to a position;

For whatever reasons they are stating that we should register natural persons in TÖR, but we don’t have a relationship that could fit the definition of “employment.” The key thing -i think- is economic interest. Could we show that the couriers are not acting in our economic interest?

Tis could be service agreement (either _töövõtu- or käsundusleping_). But it isn’t clear to me what is the part that Bolt is playing in their mind.

We can argue that the delivery service is optional and it’s up to the client to decide how they want to receive the order. The courier is an optional service and is ordered by the customer. Customer is the person providing the work, courier is the person performing the work. For Bolt it doesn’t matter if the courier is associated with the order or not - we still generate revenue (please do not quote me).

This seems to be some trap they are trying to lure us into - we provide the argumentation and they expand the meaning and state that all platform work should be registered in TÖR. 

We could state that the only point of reference is the proposed directive regarding platform work. And in accordance with the indicators given:

* we don't set the actual remuneration - it is dependant on the deliveries completed.
* we don't have binding rules for the couriers regarding their uniforms, way of transportation, communication;
* we don't have constant surveillance on the courier's KPIs;
* we don't impose any restraints regarding the time the services are provided;
* we don't impose any restrictions on competition.

