{color:#4c9aff}Dear Emilie,{color}

{color:#4c9aff}We attentively went through Bolt team’s clear notes. We naturally understand the underlying reasoning which, by the way, makes perfect sense and fits your bargaining position.{color}

{color:#4c9aff}Having said this, the notes seem to miss one point that we had the occasion to highlight: general law provides for the power of the City Council to determine pick-up/drop-off points and the concession agreement – in spite of not explicitly preventing ANA from regulating this matter – is not clear in attributing this power to ANA. This must be played against two basic legal principles: a) {color}{color:#4c9aff}_lex specalis derogat legi generali_{color}{color:#4c9aff} but unfortunately in this case the {color}{color:#4c9aff}_lex specialis_{color}{color:#4c9aff} is not clear; and b) the field of law at stake is administrative law and in the domain of public law – as opposed to private law -, only what is explicitly provided for is allowed. Thus, there is no legal presumption that the concession agreement provides ANA with such power and in case of ambiguity, one must assume that {color}{color:#4c9aff}_lex specialis_{color}{color:#4c9aff} does {color}{color:#4c9aff}+not+{color}{color:#4c9aff} derogate from {color}{color:#4c9aff}_lex generalis_{color}{color:#4c9aff}.{color}

{color:#4c9aff}This is the reason why back in August we suggested to consult the City Council to ascertain how in practice they (and possibly ANA) have interpreted this lack of clarity in the concession agreement. We see limited usefulness in a legal memo containing an opinion which may be easily contradicted by an established practice and this may actually play against the credibility of your bargaining position. In a grey area, the established practice will play a decisive role. Consulting the City Council will allow in the best scenario to strengthen our legal opinion, in a second best scenario may help finding inconsistencies still allowing to credibly sustain our opinion and in the worst case scenario will allow you to understand how exactly the law is being applied.{color}

{color:#4c9aff}As to your points regarding the issuance of the legal memo:{color}

# {color:#4c9aff}we are available to produce the memo along the lines of our preliminary assessment without consulting the City Council but we will have to unavoidably highlight the doubt mentioned above, as a matter of basic legal interpretation. We estimate EUR 2,700 in addition to the amount indicated hereunder in c);{color}
# {color:#4c9aff}we reiterate our suggestion of 16.08.2022 of consulting the City Council, for which you should count circa 15 working days;{color}
# {color:#4c9aff}we have spent so far EUR 1,800 (including today’s assessment of your notes and response);{color}
# {color:#4c9aff}we estimate EUR 600 for the consultation to the City Council and EUR 3,000 for the memo, in case the outcome of the consultation shows that it is feasible to sustain a credible legal position capable of supporting your bargaining position.{color}

{color:#4c9aff}Best regards,{color}

{color:#4c9aff}Miguel{color}

